IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `crtpa-nouns`
IMPORT `crtpa-seat`

-- Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 2001 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal
-- consolidation -- WORKED CASES FOR THE SEAT LAYER (crtpa-seat.l4).
--
-- Each case is a person in the seat -- a third party named in or benefiting from a contract -- stating facts and
-- asking one of the seat's questions.  Every expected value is written from the text of the Act, with the reason
-- beside it, before the file was run; none is copied from the encoding's own output.  A case marked OUTSIDE THE
-- ENCODING is one the text does not answer: its expected value is NOTHING, asserted.
-- In each call the inputs are, in order, those of the question's GIVEN in crtpa-seat.l4.


§ `Section 1 -- does the Act reach my contract?`

-- A contract of 10 May 2002 with no express provision: entered into before the end of the six months from 1 January 2002, and s 1(3) is not met, so "this Act does not apply" (s 1(2)).
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my contract` (Date 10 5 2002) FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The same contract, expressly providing for the Act: s 1(3) lifts the restriction, but the text never says in terms that the Act applies, so it does not answer.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my contract` (Date 10 5 2002) TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- A contract of 14 March 2005: outside the restricted period, so the restriction says nothing; the text does not say the Act applies.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my contract` (Date 14 3 2005) FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  1 July 2002 is the day the text does not decide: whether the six months end on 30 June or 1 July depends on a count of months from a day that the Interpretation Act does not give.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my contract` (Date 1 7 2002) FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- 10 May 2002, no express provision: before the end of the period and (3) not met, so the restriction applies (s 1(2)).
#ASSERT `Does the restriction on the Act's application apply to my contract` (Date 10 5 2002) FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- 14 March 2005 is after the end of the period, so the restriction does not apply.
#ASSERT `Does the restriction on the Act's application apply to my contract` (Date 14 3 2005) FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A contract of 20 December 2001 cannot use s 1(3)(a) (entered into on or after 1 January 2002), even if it expressly provides for the Act.
#ASSERT `Does the restriction on the Act's application apply to my contract` (Date 20 12 2001) TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  1 July 2002 without an express provision: the text does not decide whether it falls inside the six months.
#ASSERT `Does the restriction on the Act's application apply to my contract` (Date 1 7 2002) FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 1(3): entered into on or after 1 January 2002 and expressly providing for the Act's application: the restriction does not apply.
#ASSERT `Is the restriction on the Act's application lifted for my contract` (Date 10 5 2002) TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Without the express provision, s 1(3) does not lift the restriction and says nothing further.
#ASSERT `Is the restriction on the Act's application lifted for my contract` (Date 10 5 2002) FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 2 -- may I enforce a term myself?`

-- s 2(1): "a person who is not a party to a contract (called in this Act a third party)".
#ASSERT `Am I a third party to the contract` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A party to the contract is not a third party.
#ASSERT `Am I a third party to the contract` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not a party, and the contract expressly provides that I may enforce the term (s 2(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` FALSE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Not a party, no express provision, but the term purports to confer a benefit on me and nothing suggests the parties did not intend it to be enforceable by me (s 2(1)(b)).
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` FALSE FALSE TRUE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Same, but on a proper construction the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by me: s 2(2) takes (1)(b) out, and there is no express provision.
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` FALSE FALSE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Whether the parties did not intend it is a judgment of the contract's meaning that I cannot make: the Act does not answer.
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` FALSE FALSE TRUE `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- No express provision and no benefit conferred: neither route of s 2(1).
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` FALSE FALSE FALSE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A party to the contract is not a third party, so s 2(1) gives nothing.
#ASSERT `May I enforce a term of the contract in my own right` TRUE TRUE TRUE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A benefit purported to be conferred, and nothing against the parties' intention: s 2(1)(b) is made out.
#ASSERT `Does the term purport to confer a benefit on me and not fall within the intention exception` TRUE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A benefit, but the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by me: s 2(2) takes it out.
#ASSERT `Does the term purport to confer a benefit on me and not fall within the intention exception` TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- I cannot say whether the parties so intended: not answered.
#ASSERT `Does the term purport to confer a benefit on me and not fall within the intention exception` TRUE `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(2): if on a proper construction it appears the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable, subsection (1)(b) does not apply.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(2) take the term out of section 2(1)(b)` Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- If it does not appear so, s 2(2) does not take (1)(b) out.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(2) take the term out of section 2(1)(b)` No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not stated: not answered.
#ASSERT `Does section 2(2) take the term out of section 2(1)(b)` `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(3): the third party must be expressly identified by name, as a member of a class or as answering a particular description.
#ASSERT `Does the contract identify me as section 2(3) requires` `expressly, by name` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A member of a class is a way s 2(3) lists.
#ASSERT `Does the contract identify me as section 2(3) requires` `expressly, as a member of a class` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Identified by implication only is not "expressly identified" (read as the text's no, judgment call QB).
#ASSERT `Does the contract identify me as section 2(3) requires` `by implication only` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not identified at all is not "expressly identified" (judgment call QB).
#ASSERT `Does the contract identify me as section 2(3) requires` `not identified at all` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 2(3): the third party "need not be in existence when the contract is entered into": a company not yet incorporated may still be a third party.
#ASSERT `Must I have been in existence when the contract was entered into` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Existence is not required whether or not I was in existence.
#ASSERT `Must I have been in existence when the contract was entered into` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 2(4) forbids a right to enforce otherwise than subject to the other relevant terms; if my enforcing is in accordance with them the text does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right to enforce otherwise than subject to the contract's other terms` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- If my enforcing would not be in accordance with the other relevant terms, section 2 does not confer the right (s 2(4)).
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right to enforce otherwise than subject to the contract's other terms` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 2(5): I have a right to enforce the term (express provision), and the remedy would have been available to a party: it is available to me.
#ASSERT `Is the remedy I want available to me` FALSE TRUE FALSE No TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The remedy would not have been available to a party: the text does not grant it, and does not say it is refused.
#ASSERT `Is the remedy I want available to me` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- I am a party, so section 2 gives me no right and s 2(5) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Is the remedy I want available to me` TRUE TRUE TRUE No TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(5): for the purpose of exercising my right to enforce the term (express provision), the rules relating to damages, injunctions, specific performance and other remedy apply accordingly.
#ASSERT `Do the rules on damages, injunctions and other remedies apply to my remedy` FALSE TRUE FALSE No EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  I am a party, so I have no right under section 2; the parenthesis speaks only of a third party's right to enforce.
#ASSERT `Do the rules on damages, injunctions and other remedies apply to my remedy` TRUE TRUE TRUE No EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(5): the remedy "must not be refused on the ground that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer".
#ASSERT `May the promisor refuse me a remedy because I am a volunteer` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A refusal on another ground: s 2(5) says nothing about it.
#ASSERT `May the promisor refuse me a remedy because I am a volunteer` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(6): where a term excludes or limits liability, references to the third party enforcing it mean availing of the exclusion or limitation.
#ASSERT `Is a reference to my enforcing the term to be read as my availing myself of an exclusion or limitation` `a term that excludes or limits liability in relation to a matter` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Any other term: the reference keeps its ordinary sense.
#ASSERT `Is a reference to my enforcing the term to be read as my availing myself of an exclusion or limitation` `any other term` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 2(6).
#ASSERT `What a reference to my enforcing the term is to be read as` `a term that excludes or limits liability in relation to a matter` EQUALS JUST `availing of the exclusion or limitation`
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  The text gives no other meaning to the reference.
#ASSERT `What a reference to my enforcing the term is to be read as` `any other term` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(7): "promisee" means the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor.
#ASSERT `Is the party I am asking about the promisee` TRUE `the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The term is not one a third party can enforce, so the definition of s 2(7) does not apply.
#ASSERT `Is the party I am asking about the promisee` FALSE `the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 2(7): "promisor" means the party against whom the term is enforceable by the third party.
#ASSERT `Is the party I am asking about the promisor` TRUE `the party against whom the term is enforceable by the third party` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The promisee is not the promisor.
#ASSERT `Is the party I am asking about the promisor` TRUE `the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor` EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Section 3 -- may the parties cancel or change my right?`

-- s 3(1)(a): I have communicated my assent to the promisor.
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(1)(b): the promisor is aware that I have relied on the term.
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` FALSE TRUE No FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(1)(c): the promisor can reasonably be expected to have foreseen that I would rely and I have in fact relied.
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` FALSE FALSE Yes TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- None of (a), (b) and (c).
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` FALSE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- (c) needs both foreseeability and reliance; I relied but cannot say whether the promisor could foresee it: not answered.
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` FALSE FALSE `I am not sure` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- (c) needs reliance, and I have not relied, so (c) fails whatever the promisor could foresee.
#ASSERT `Has one of the circumstances in section 3(1) arisen` FALSE FALSE `I am not sure` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(1): the circumstances apply "whether or not the third party has knowledge of its precise terms": knowledge is no condition.
#ASSERT `Is knowledge of the precise terms a condition of section 3(1)(b) and (c)` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Same, if I do not know the precise terms.
#ASSERT `Is knowledge of the precise terms a condition of section 3(1)(b) and (c)` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(2)(a): the assent may be by words.
#ASSERT `May I give my assent in this form` `words` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(2)(a): the assent may be by conduct.
#ASSERT `May I give my assent in this form` `conduct` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  The text lists words and conduct; it does not say whether silence suffices.
#ASSERT `May I give my assent in this form` `silence or inaction` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(2)(b): an assent sent by post or other means is not regarded as communicated until received; I sent it and the promisor has not received it.
#ASSERT `Can my assent not yet be regarded as communicated to the promisor` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Once the promisor has received it, the bar no longer applies.
#ASSERT `Can my assent not yet be regarded as communicated to the promisor` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Sent but not received: not regarded as communicated.
#ASSERT `Has my assent been communicated to the promisor` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Received: the text stops speaking of it, and does not say in terms that it has been communicated.
#ASSERT `Has my assent been communicated to the promisor` TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(1): I have a right (express provision); the parties rescind; I have not consented; I have communicated my assent (circumstance (a)): they may not.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A variation that alters my entitlement, with the promisor aware of my reliance (circumstance (b)).
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `vary the contract in such a way as to extinguish or alter the third party's entitlement` FALSE FALSE TRUE No FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- "Without the third party's consent": I have consented, and the bar does not speak.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` TRUE TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  A variation that neither extinguishes nor alters my entitlement is outside the bar.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `vary the contract in some other way` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- None of the circumstances (a), (b) and (c) has arisen: the bar does not apply.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- I am a party, so I have no right under section 2 and the bar of s 3(1) does not apply to me as a third party.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent` TRUE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(3): no express term of the contract, so s 3(1) stands.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent given the contract's own terms` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE `no such express term` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(3)(a): the contract lets the parties rescind or vary without my consent, so s 3(1) does not bar them.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent given the contract's own terms` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE TRUE FALSE No FALSE `a term under which the parties may rescind or vary without the third party's consent` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(3)(b): the contract requires my consent in circumstances it specifies instead of those in (a), (b) and (c), and they exist: the consent is needed although (a), (b) and (c) did not arise.
#ASSERT `Are the parties barred from rescinding or varying the contract without my consent given the contract's own terms` FALSE TRUE FALSE No `rescind the contract` FALSE FALSE FALSE No FALSE `a term requiring the third party's consent in circumstances specified in the contract instead of those in section 3(1)(a), (b) and (c)` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(4): my consent is required, the parties have applied, and the court is satisfied that my whereabouts cannot reasonably be ascertained.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with my consent` TRUE TRUE Yes `I am not sure` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Neither ground (whereabouts, mental incapacity) is made out.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with my consent` TRUE TRUE No No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- No application by the parties to the contract.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with my consent` TRUE FALSE Yes No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  The court's satisfaction has not been stated: not answered.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with my consent` TRUE TRUE `I am not sure` `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(5): a consent that may be required under (1)(c), the parties have applied, and the court is satisfied that it cannot reasonably be ascertained whether I relied.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with a consent of mine under section 3(1)(c)` TRUE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The consent is not one that may be required under (1)(c): s 3(5) does not speak.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal dispense with a consent of mine under section 3(1)(c)` FALSE TRUE Yes EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 3(6): where the court dispenses with the consent, it may impose such conditions as it thinks fit.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal impose conditions when it dispenses with my consent` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Where it has not dispensed with the consent, there is nothing to attach a condition to.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal impose conditions when it dispenses with my consent` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(6): including a condition requiring the payment of compensation to the third party.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal require compensation to be paid to me` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No dispensation, no condition.
#ASSERT `May the court or arbitral tribunal require compensation to be paid to me` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 3(7): exercisable by the General Division of the High Court.
#ASSERT `May this body exercise the jurisdiction to dispense with my consent` `the General Division of the High Court` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 3(7): and by a District Court.
#ASSERT `May this body exercise the jurisdiction to dispense with my consent` `a District Court` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  s 3(7) names those two courts and does not speak of others.
#ASSERT `May this body exercise the jurisdiction to dispense with my consent` `another court` EQUALS NOTHING
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  s 3(7) speaks of courts; the arbitral tribunal's jurisdiction is in s 3(4) to (6).
#ASSERT `May this body exercise the jurisdiction to dispense with my consent` `an arbitral tribunal` EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 4 -- what defences does the promisor have against me?`

-- s 4(1): subsections (2) to (5) apply where proceedings for the enforcement of a term are brought by a third party in reliance on section 2.
#ASSERT `Do the promisor's defences in section 4 apply in my proceedings` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Not proceedings by a third party in reliance on section 2: they do not apply.
#ASSERT `Do the promisor's defences in section 4 apply in my proceedings` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(2): the matter arises from the contract and is relevant to the term, and would have been available against the promisee: available to the promisor.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(2)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 4(5): an express term takes the matter away, so (2) is subject to it.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(2)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The matter does not arise from or in connection with the contract: s 4(2) gives the promisor no defence on these facts (not "the promisor has no defence at all"; judgment call G17).
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(2)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not proceedings by a third party in reliance on section 2 (s 4(1)).
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(2)` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(3): an express term of the contract provides for the matter to be available, and it would have been available against the promisee.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(3)` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No express term providing for it: (3)'s "if" is unmet.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(3)` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(4)(a): the matter would have been available by defence or set-off against me had I been a party, and no express term takes it away.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(a)` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 4(5): an express term takes it away.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(a)` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- It would not have been available against me as a party: s 4(4)(a) gives the promisor no defence on these facts (judgment call G17).
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(a)` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(4)(b): a matter not arising from the contract, available by counterclaim against me had I been a party.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(b)` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 4(5): an express term takes it away.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(b)` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The matter arises from the contract, so s 4(4)(b) gives the promisor no defence on these facts (judgment call G17).
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor under section 4(4)(b)` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(5): (2) and (4) are subject to any express term of the contract as to the matters that are not to be available.
#ASSERT `Is the promisor's defence subject to an express term that takes it away` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No such express term: the subject-to speaks only of such a term.
#ASSERT `Is the promisor's defence subject to an express term that takes it away` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Available under (2).
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor by way of defence or set-off, taking section 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) together` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Not under (2) (the matter does not arise from the contract), but under (3): an express term provides for it.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor by way of defence or set-off, taking section 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) together` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Under (4)(a): available against me as a party.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor by way of defence or set-off, taking section 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) together` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- None of the three grants covers the matter: s 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) give the promisor no defence on these facts (not "the promisor has no defence at all").
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor by way of defence or set-off, taking section 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) together` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not proceedings by a third party in reliance on section 2: none of (2) to (5) applies.
#ASSERT `Is the matter available to the promisor by way of defence or set-off, taking section 4(2), (3) and (4)(a) together` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 4(6): proceedings are brought against me, I seek to enforce a term in reliance on section 2, and I could not have done so had I been a party: I may not.
#ASSERT `Am I barred from enforcing the term in the proceedings brought against me` TRUE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- I could have done so as a party: the bar's "if" is unmet.
#ASSERT `Am I barred from enforcing the term in the proceedings brought against me` TRUE TRUE No EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- I cannot say whether I could have done so as a party: not answered.
#ASSERT `Am I barred from enforcing the term in the proceedings brought against me` TRUE TRUE `I am not sure` EQUALS NOTHING
-- No proceedings are brought against me: s 4(6)'s "where" is unmet.
#ASSERT `Am I barred from enforcing the term in the proceedings brought against me` FALSE TRUE Yes EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Sections 5 and 6 -- the promisee, and double liability`

-- s 5: "Section 2 does not affect any right of the promisee to enforce any term of the contract."
#ASSERT `Does section 2 affect the promisee's right to enforce a term` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A right that is not the promisee's: s 5 does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 affect the promisee's right to enforce a term` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 6: a term is enforceable by me under section 2, the promisee has recovered a sum in respect of my loss, and I bring proceedings in reliance on section 2: the court or tribunal must reduce my award.
#ASSERT `Must the court or arbitral tribunal reduce my award because the promisee has recovered a sum` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- s 6(b): the promisee has recovered a sum in respect of the expense of making good the promisor's default to me.
#ASSERT `Must the court or arbitral tribunal reduce my award because the promisee has recovered a sum` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The promisee has recovered nothing of either kind: s 6's "where" is unmet.
#ASSERT `Must the court or arbitral tribunal reduce my award because the promisee has recovered a sum` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  The proceedings are not ones brought in reliance on section 2 by me: the duty of s 6 is not in play.
#ASSERT `Must the court or arbitral tribunal reduce my award because the promisee has recovered a sum` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- An award of $10,000 reduced by the $2,500 the tribunal thinks appropriate.
#ASSERT `What will my award be after the reduction` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 10000 TRUE 2500 EQUALS JUST 7500
-- The tribunal decides that no reduction is appropriate.
#ASSERT `What will my award be after the reduction` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 10000 TRUE 0 EQUALS JUST 10000
-- OUTSIDE THE ENCODING.  "To such extent as it thinks appropriate": the extent is the tribunal's judgment and has not been made.
#ASSERT `What will my award be after the reduction` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 10000 FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- A reduction by more than the award is not one the text describes.
#ASSERT `What will my award be after the reduction` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 10000 TRUE 10001 EQUALS NOTHING
-- The duty does not arise, so no reduced award is stated.
#ASSERT `What will my award be after the reduction` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 10000 TRUE 2500 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Section 7 -- is my contract one of the exceptions?`

-- s 7(1): section 2 confers no right in the case of a contract on a bill of exchange, promissory note or other negotiable instrument.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract on a negotiable instrument` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Another contract: s 7(1) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract on a negotiable instrument` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(2): no right in the case of a contract binding on a company and its members under section 39 of the Companies Act 1967.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a company's constitution` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Another contract: s 7(2) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a company's constitution` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(3): a registration document of a limited liability partnership.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a limited liability partnership's documents` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 7(3): a limited liability partnership agreement.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a limited liability partnership's documents` FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Another contract: s 7(3) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a limited liability partnership's documents` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(4): no right to enforce a term of a contract of employment against an employee.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right to enforce a term of a contract of employment against an employee` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A contract of employment, but I am not seeking to enforce it against an employee: s 7(4) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right to enforce a term of a contract of employment against an employee` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(5)(a): a contract of carriage evidenced by a bill of lading is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea: no right.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract of carriage` TRUE Yes No `carriage by sea` No FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The exception to the bar: I seek only to avail myself of an exclusion or limitation of liability; a lifted bar grants nothing here (judgment call QA).
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract of carriage` TRUE Yes No `carriage by sea` No TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 7(5)(b): carriage by road subject to the rules of the appropriate international transport convention: no right.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract of carriage` TRUE No No `carriage of goods by road` Yes FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Carriage by road not subject to the convention's rules, not a sea contract: s 7(5) does not speak.
#ASSERT `Does section 2 give me a right under a contract of carriage` TRUE No No `carriage of goods by road` No FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Not a contract of carriage: s 7(5) does not speak.
